WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.625
Welcome to Module 15 of the Non-Person
Identity learning path: your first G M S A.

2
00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:08.062
First you set up the forest, once.

3
00:00:08.062 --> 00:00:11.424
Then you create a new G M
S A for a new application.

4
00:00:11.424 --> 00:00:16.757
It teaches two parts of the companion
guide Service Accounts: set it up once, and

5
00:00:16.757 --> 00:00:21.925
a new G M S A for a new workload, with
Chapters 6 and 12 of the developer workbook.

6
00:00:21.925 --> 00:00:25.120
Take it after Module
1 and before Module 8.

7
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.232
Why this module?

8
00:00:30.232 --> 00:00:34.392
Every other lab in this path starts
from an account that already exists:

9
00:00:34.392 --> 00:00:39.171
a user account used as a service, a standalone
managed service account, or a secret.

10
00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:43.108
You move its S P Ns, run in
parallel, then disable it.

11
00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:45.045
This module starts from nothing.

12
00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:50.424
First the forest gets what every G
M S A needs, once: a K D S root key,

13
00:00:50.424 --> 00:00:55.908
the O Us, a group that may create G M
S As, and the pipeline's own G M S A.

14
00:00:55.908 --> 00:00:59.445
Then a new application gets
its G M S A from the first day,

15
00:00:59.445 --> 00:01:03.298
by the managed service account
standard, with no S P N to move,

16
00:01:03.298 --> 00:01:06.207
no parallel run and no
old account to disable.

17
00:01:06.207 --> 00:01:09.693
That's why it comes before
Module 8: Module 8 converts the

18
00:01:09.693 --> 00:01:14.020
accounts that already exist, into
the forest this module sets up.

19
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.647
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

20
00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:22.895
which takes about sixty
minutes with Lab 1.

21
00:01:22.895 --> 00:01:26.182
You'll set up a forest for
G M S As in the right order.

22
00:01:26.182 --> 00:01:29.453
You'll say when the backdated
K D S key is allowed,

23
00:01:29.453 --> 00:01:31.633
and why a forest has only one key.

24
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:36.395
You'll follow a new workload's G M S A
from the request to a working service.

25
00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:39.737
You'll decide whether a new
G M S A is outbound-only,

26
00:01:39.737 --> 00:01:42.995
and register an S P N when
the workload needs one.

27
00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:45.488
And you'll run Chapter 12's request check

28
00:01:45.488 --> 00:01:48.208
and Chapter 6's one-account
plan on fixtures.

29
00:01:48.208 --> 00:01:52.445
Before you start, finish Module 1
and read the standard's settings.

30
00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:58.600
You need PowerShell 7.1 or later with Pester
5, and no domain or tenant for Lab 1.

31
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.313
Unit 1 is the setup,
and the order matters.

32
00:02:05.313 --> 00:02:08.962
First, the K D S root key, one per forest.

33
00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:13.579
A Domain Admin or Enterprise Admin
creates it on a domain controller,

34
00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:18.278
confirms it with the K D S service's
event forty oh oh four, and then waits:

35
00:02:18.278 --> 00:02:22.166
domain controllers wait up to
ten hours before they allow

36
00:02:22.166 --> 00:02:25.325
a G M S A to be created, so
the key replicates first.

37
00:02:25.325 --> 00:02:28.427
Second, the O Us: one for
managed service accounts,

38
00:02:28.427 --> 00:02:32.150
and a holding O U for the disabled
ones a migration retires.

39
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:37.400
Third, the creators group, and the right
to create G M S A objects on that O U.

40
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:43.094
Microsoft Learn names the right but gives
no command, so the guide uses D S A C L S,

41
00:02:43.094 --> 00:02:47.988
which is design guidance: run it in
a test domain first, then check it.

42
00:02:47.988 --> 00:02:51.557
Fourth, the pipeline's own
G M S A, made once by hand,

43
00:02:51.557 --> 00:02:55.126
by the standard, and then
the group's only member.

44
00:02:55.126 --> 00:02:59.790
After that, nobody needs Domain
Admins to create a G M S A.

45
00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.640
The K D S root key deserves its own slide.

46
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.362
In any forest, you create it with Add K
D S Root Key, dash Effective Immediately,

47
00:03:12.362 --> 00:03:16.852
and then you wait, up to ten hours,
before a G M S A can be created.

48
00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:19.788
Learn's K D S page offers
a key backdated ten hours,

49
00:03:19.788 --> 00:03:23.383
but only for a test environment
with one domain controller,

50
00:03:23.383 --> 00:03:26.978
and Learn's containers page says
not to use it in production.

51
00:03:26.978 --> 00:03:28.790
So: never in production.

52
00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.265
And create only one.

53
00:03:30.265 --> 00:03:35.580
Learn says more than one key makes G M S As
start failing after a password rotation,

54
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.674
and a deleted and re-created
key can stay cached,

55
00:03:38.674 --> 00:03:42.720
so the K D C has to be restarted
on every domain controller.

56
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.420
Unit 2 follows one new application,
timesheet, a service on two hosts.

57
00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.295
Its owner asks for a G M S A
with no account to supersede.

58
00:03:55.295 --> 00:03:59.790
SailPoint approves, and Chapter 12
checks every field; the row it writes

59
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:04.133
for Chapter 6 names the new G M S A
itself, because there's no old account.

60
00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:09.323
The pipeline runs Chapter 6's one-account
plan: the service-account O U,

61
00:04:09.323 --> 00:04:14.332
A E S two fifty-six, thirty days, its
own hosts' group, and outbound-only.

62
00:04:14.332 --> 00:04:19.421
Each host restarts to pick up its group,
then installs the G M S A and tests it.

63
00:04:19.421 --> 00:04:21.350
Then the service is switched to it,

64
00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.520
with a blank password, and
given only the rights it needs.

65
00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.806
And look at what's skipped:
no delegation to carry,

66
00:04:27.806 --> 00:04:31.995
no S P N to move, no thirty-day
parallel run, no account to disable.

67
00:04:31.995 --> 00:04:36.760
Those steps exist to retire an old
account, and a new application has none.

68
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.160
Does the new G M S A need an S P N?

69
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.549
Timesheet is a service
that only calls out,

70
00:04:45.549 --> 00:04:48.198
so nothing authenticates
to it with Kerberos.

71
00:04:48.198 --> 00:04:52.385
Its G M S A is outbound-only,
which is the standard's default.

72
00:04:52.385 --> 00:04:57.191
Visitor is an I I S site that
browsers reach with Kerberos,

73
00:04:57.191 --> 00:05:00.873
so its G M S A needs the S
P N H T T P slash visitor.

74
00:05:00.873 --> 00:05:04.648
It isn't outbound-only, and the
reason is recorded in the request.

75
00:05:04.648 --> 00:05:09.246
A person registers the S P N once nothing
else holds it, with set S P N dash S,

76
00:05:09.246 --> 00:05:12.986
which Learn says checks for a
duplicate before it adds one.

77
00:05:12.986 --> 00:05:17.050
Then set S P N dash L
confirms it's on the G M S A.

78
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:21.737
Try it.

79
00:05:21.737 --> 00:05:25.626
Lab 1 runs in the non-person
identity folder, with no domain.

80
00:05:25.626 --> 00:05:30.803
Step one runs the kit's tests of Microsoft
Learn's own K D S and G M S A procedures,

81
00:05:30.803 --> 00:05:34.338
against stand-ins for the
Active Directory cmdlets.

82
00:05:34.338 --> 00:05:37.325
Six pass, each a fact about Learn's pages.

83
00:05:37.325 --> 00:05:40.317
Get K D S Root Key returns
the key's value itself,

84
00:05:40.317 --> 00:05:43.387
so only an admin who would
create the key runs it.

85
00:05:43.387 --> 00:05:47.462
The backdated key is for a test
environment with one domain controller.

86
00:05:47.462 --> 00:05:51.897
Learn's G M S A sample sets its
retrievers at creation, and sets no

87
00:05:51.897 --> 00:05:57.099
Kerberos encryption type, which is why
the standard adds A E S two fifty-six.

88
00:05:57.099 --> 00:06:01.390
And installing the G M S A on
each host is a person's step.

89
00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.661
Step two runs Chapter
12's request check on

90
00:06:07.661 --> 00:06:10.203
three approved requests for new workloads.

91
00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:15.277
The domain has no G M S A yet, so
there's nothing to certify or retire.

92
00:06:15.277 --> 00:06:18.303
Two requests pass: timesheet and visitor.

93
00:06:18.303 --> 00:06:19.315
One is held.

94
00:06:19.315 --> 00:06:20.853
Step three reads why.

95
00:06:20.853 --> 00:06:24.058
Its name is nineteen characters
before the dollar sign,

96
00:06:24.058 --> 00:06:25.940
and the standard allows fifteen.

97
00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.703
And it names only one owner, where
the standard needs exactly two.

98
00:06:29.703 --> 00:06:32.690
SailPoint approved it, and
the code still holds it.

99
00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.665
The approvers decide on
the purpose and the risk;

100
00:06:35.665 --> 00:06:40.360
the code checks the standard, field
by field, before anything is created.

101
00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.710
Step four reads what Chapter
12 hands to Chapter 6.

102
00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:50.325
Two rows, one for each
request that passed,

103
00:06:50.325 --> 00:06:53.147
each with its request
and its change record.

104
00:06:53.147 --> 00:06:55.011
Look at the first two columns.

105
00:06:55.011 --> 00:06:57.985
In a migration, the first
one is the old account.

106
00:06:57.985 --> 00:07:01.822
Here it's the new G M S A itself,
because there's no old account.

107
00:07:01.822 --> 00:07:04.631
And the retrievers file
gives each G M S A its

108
00:07:04.631 --> 00:07:07.900
own domain local group of
the hosts that run it.

109
00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.185
Step five builds Chapter
6's one-account plan,

110
00:07:15.185 --> 00:07:18.575
the plan for a G M S A
that replaces no account.

111
00:07:18.575 --> 00:07:24.725
One action: create G M S A, run by the
pipeline, with its request and change record.

112
00:07:24.725 --> 00:07:29.485
Its precheck names two things:
the K D S root key must be ready,

113
00:07:29.485 --> 00:07:31.913
and the G M S A must not exist yet.

114
00:07:31.913 --> 00:07:36.842
The parameters are the standard's: the
service-account O U, the hosts' group

115
00:07:36.842 --> 00:07:41.525
as the only retriever, A E S two
fifty-six, thirty days, and outbound-only.

116
00:07:41.525 --> 00:07:44.309
A name that breaks the
standard, like step three's,

117
00:07:44.309 --> 00:07:46.990
makes the plan throw instead of planning.

118
00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:55.265
Step six dry-runs the plan, as the
pipeline, with its approved hash,

119
00:07:55.265 --> 00:08:00.077
in a copy of the fictional forest before
its setup, with no K D S root key.

120
00:08:00.077 --> 00:08:04.765
The create is skipped, and the detail
says why: K D S root key missing.

121
00:08:04.765 --> 00:08:06.790
That's why Unit 1 comes first.

122
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:11.965
Step seven runs the same plan, with the
same hash, in the forest after its setup.

123
00:08:11.965 --> 00:08:15.040
Now the pipeline would send
New A D Service Account.

124
00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.902
Nothing is sent in either
run: they're dry runs.

125
00:08:17.902 --> 00:08:21.570
Step eight runs the module's
nine tests, and cleans up.

126
00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:29.303
Lab 2 does it for real, in a test
domain with one domain controller,

127
00:08:29.303 --> 00:08:33.458
never production, as a Domain Admin,
with a member server for the host.

128
00:08:33.458 --> 00:08:36.894
Nobody has run it yet, so no
step's output has been seen.

129
00:08:36.894 --> 00:08:39.852
Steps one to four are the
setup: the K D S root key,

130
00:08:39.852 --> 00:08:45.299
backdated only in this single domain controller
test domain and only if none exists,

131
00:08:45.299 --> 00:08:49.657
the two O Us, the creators group with
its right, and the pipeline's G M S A.

132
00:08:49.657 --> 00:08:54.432
Step five creates the timesheet G M S
A by hand, the way the pipeline would.

133
00:08:54.432 --> 00:08:56.782
Step six, on the host after a restart,

134
00:08:56.782 --> 00:09:00.508
installs and tests it, and sets
a test service to run as it.

135
00:09:00.508 --> 00:09:03.676
Step seven backs out the
G M S A and its group,

136
00:09:03.676 --> 00:09:06.482
and keeps the setup, which is made once.

137
00:09:06.482 --> 00:09:09.488
In any domain with more
than one domain controller,

138
00:09:09.488 --> 00:09:12.510
use Effective Immediately
and wait ten hours.

139
00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:17.910
The federal requirement.

140
00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.464
The standard's steps each
name what they support,

141
00:09:20.464 --> 00:09:23.785
and these are the rows this
module's labs produce evidence for.

142
00:09:23.785 --> 00:09:28.340
The request check in S1 supports A
C two, Account Management, I A four,

143
00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:36.623
Identifier Management, and C M eight, System
Component Inventory, in NIST SP 800-53.

144
00:09:36.623 --> 00:09:41.894
The approval in S3 supports A C two, C
M three, Configuration Change Control,

145
00:09:41.894 --> 00:09:44.228
and A C five, Separation of Duties,

146
00:09:44.228 --> 00:09:48.635
with the request and change
carried on every write in the plan.

147
00:09:48.635 --> 00:09:52.769
The create in S4 supports A C
six, Least Privilege, I A five,

148
00:09:52.769 --> 00:09:57.442
Authenticator Management, C M
three and A C two enhancement one,

149
00:09:57.442 --> 00:10:02.385
automated account management, with
the plan's hash and its results.

150
00:10:02.385 --> 00:10:05.447
And the install in S5 supports A C six,

151
00:10:05.447 --> 00:10:10.447
A C six enhancement five, and C
M six, Configuration Settings.

152
00:10:10.447 --> 00:10:15.072
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

153
00:10:15.072 --> 00:10:19.172
A row provides evidence for a
control; the assessor decides.

154
00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.020
Check yourself with two questions.

155
00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:29.220
First: when may you use the K D
S root key backdated ten hours?

156
00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:31.719
The answer is b: only
in a test environment

157
00:10:31.719 --> 00:10:34.658
with one domain controller,
never in production.

158
00:10:34.658 --> 00:10:39.995
Second: a new I I S site needs
the S P N H T T P slash visitor.

159
00:10:39.995 --> 00:10:41.495
What does the standard say?

160
00:10:41.495 --> 00:10:47.332
The answer is b: create the G M S A without
the outbound-only restriction, record

161
00:10:47.332 --> 00:10:50.525
why, and register the S P
N with set S P N dash S,

162
00:10:50.525 --> 00:10:53.170
which checks for duplicates first.

163
00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:56.833
Outbound-only is for a
G M S A with no S P N.

164
00:10:56.833 --> 00:10:58.951
The module page has three more questions,

165
00:10:58.951 --> 00:11:01.450
each linked to the
section that answers it.

166
00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:06.275
To sum up.

167
00:11:06.275 --> 00:11:11.802
A forest is set up for G M S As once: one
K D S root key, aged ten hours, the O Us,

168
00:11:11.802 --> 00:11:17.575
the creators group with its delegated
right, and the pipeline's own G M S A.

169
00:11:17.575 --> 00:11:22.825
After that, a new application's G M S A
follows the standard from the first day.

170
00:11:22.825 --> 00:11:24.331
It's requested and checked,

171
00:11:24.331 --> 00:11:28.026
planned and created by the pipeline
with the standard's settings,

172
00:11:28.026 --> 00:11:32.612
installed and tested on its hosts, and
given to the workload with a blank password.

173
00:11:32.612 --> 00:11:35.777
It's outbound-only unless
the workload needs an S P N,

174
00:11:35.777 --> 00:11:37.887
and it has nothing to migrate from.

175
00:11:37.887 --> 00:11:42.083
Next, Module 8 converts the Kerberos
accounts that already exist,

176
00:11:42.083 --> 00:11:44.030
into the forest you just set up.
